#: ../../source/guides/input-db/recognising-companies.md:1
msgid "Companies, and how they are recognised"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/input-db/recognising-companies.md:10
msgid ""
"Each company has a **name** — what ends up in the output, and the first "
"thing looked for — plus up to three kinds of evidence for finding it in "
"text that was typeset for humans:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/input-db/recognising-companies.md:13
msgid ""
"**Buds** are verbatim fragments that gate the cheap pass. The matcher "
"tries a company's regexes against a piece of text only if one of that "
"company's buds occurs in it, which is what keeps a table of hundreds of "
"rows against hundreds of companies fast. A bud is therefore about *the "
"text*, not about the name."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/input-db/recognising-companies.md:18
msgid ""
"**Regexes** are the patterns the name takes in practice — abbreviations, "
"legal suffixes, group names."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/input-db/recognising-companies.md:21
msgid ""
"**Tickers** are exchange symbols, two to six upper-case letters, attached"
" to a company and a market."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/input-db/recognising-companies.md:23
msgid "The main table is checked against the name; the additional files are not"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/input-db/recognising-companies.md:25
msgid ""
"This is the distinction that matters, and it is not a matter of file "
"shape."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/input-db/recognising-companies.md:27
msgid ""
"The `Bud` and `Regex` columns of `companies.csv` describe **the company's"
" own name**. So they are checked against it: the bud must be already "
"normalised and must actually occur in the normalised name, and the regex "
"must match that name. If you put an identifier here, it has to be "
"consistent with the name it sits next to — one that is not could only "
"ever produce a false match or none, and is rejected when the database is "
"read."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/input-db/recognising-companies.md:33
msgid ""
"The two `companies_additional_*` files are for **the other names the same"
" company genuinely goes by** — a former name, a brand, a parent group, a "
"local subsidiary, the string a particular registrar insists on printing. "
"`Alphabet` is written `Google` in half the reports that hold it. These "
"have no reason to resemble the name in `companies.csv`, so **no such "
"check is applied to them**: the only validation is that the company they "
"name exists in the main table."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/input-db/recognising-companies.md:39
msgid ""
"Do not read that as laxity. It is the point of the two files: a bud or a "
"regex that had to be contained in the official name could not express the"
" case they exist for."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/input-db/recognising-companies.md:42
msgid ""
"One check does still reach an additional **bud**, and it is a different "
"question from resembling the name: it must be **already normalised**. A "
"bud is compared verbatim against text the matcher has normalised, so "
"`ALADDIN` or `black-rock` would match nothing, ever, and say nothing "
"about it — the check is about the alphabet the comparison happens in. An "
"additional **regex** is exempt, being a pattern rather than a literal: "
"anchors, character classes and escapes have no business being normalised."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/input-db/recognising-companies.md:49
msgid ""
"Matching runs on **normalised** forms — accents, case, punctuation and "
"spacing removed, in three increasing degrees — so that `Café Fund`, `CAFE"
"  FUND` and `Cafe' Fund` are one name. The name is kept exactly as "
"written alongside the normalised form, and it is the written one that "
"reaches the output."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/input-db/recognising-companies.md:54
msgid "The name is tried first, and only where a name can end"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/input-db/recognising-companies.md:56
msgid ""
"Before any bud or regex, the matcher asks the cheap question: does the "
"text simply contain the company's name? It is a substring search and not "
"a pattern, and that is the point — it is what makes the first pass "
"survive hundreds of table rows against hundreds of companies."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/input-db/recognising-companies.md:60
msgid ""
"Containment alone, though, attributes holdings to the wrong company, and "
"the two cases that prove it come from real reports:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/input-db/recognising-companies.md
msgid "The text"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/input-db/recognising-companies.md
msgid "The company"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/input-db/recognising-companies.md
msgid "Why bare containment matches"
msgstr ""

