#: ../../source/guides/input-db/recognising-companies.md:68
msgid ""
"So the occurrence has to be **delimited**: no letter immediately before "
"it, and none immediately after. A digit is a perfectly good boundary, and"
" so is punctuation — reports write `3M 2029`, `SSE 4.75% 2031`, `ENI-"
"SPA`, and a rule demanding a space would lose all three. Only a letter "
"touching the occurrence means the page is saying a longer word."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/input-db/recognising-companies.md:73
msgid ""
"One subtlety is worth knowing, because it decides whether a holding is "
"found. Normalisation **erases** `.`, `/`, `'` and their kind rather than "
"spacing them, so `AMAZON.COM INC` becomes `amazoncom inc` and `Amazon` "
"would appear to run into a letter the report never wrote next to it. The "
"text is therefore read **both ways** — as normalised, and with that "
"punctuation restored as a separator — and a delimited occurrence in "
"either reading is a match. `AMAZON.COM INC`, `BOOKING.COM` and `L'OREAL "
"SA` are found; `Other Assets`, which has no punctuation to split on, "
"stays rejected."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/input-db/recognising-companies.md:81
msgid ""
"This applies to the name only. Buds are gates for a regex that then "
"decides, and the regexes themselves say where their own boundaries are — "
"`\\balphabet\\b` in the table above is doing exactly that job by hand."
msgstr ""

